Recognizing and interpreting idiomatic expressions and figures of speech in natural language can be a challenging task for machine learning algorithms. However, there are several techniques that can be employed in Go to develop such an algorithm:
1. Use of Natural Language Processing (NLP) libraries such as GoNLP, GoStanfordNLP, or GoSpacy to extract linguistic features from text.
2. Utilizing machine learning models such as Support Vector Machines (SVMs), Random Forests, or Neural Networks to classify idiomatic expressions and figures of speech.
3. Incorporating contextual and cultural factors by training the algorithm on diverse datasets that capture variations in meaning across different populations and regions.
4. Leveraging crowd-sourced data to enhance the algorithm's capacity for linguistic analysis and prediction.
To ensure that the algorithm can detect and generate humor and sarcasm in text, sentiment analysis techniques can be employed. These techniques involve analyzing the sentiment of the text and identifying whether it is positive, negative, or neutral. Humorous and sarcastic expressions often have a negative sentiment, and the algorithm can be trained to identify such expressions.
Here is some sample Go code that demonstrates how to employ these techniques:
```
package main
import (
	"fmt"
	"github.com/jbrukh/bayesian"
	"github.com/kljensen/snowball"
	"github.com/kljensen/snowball/english"
	"github.com/kljensen/snowball/porter"
	"github.com/kljensen/snowball/spanish"
	"strings"
)
func main() {
	// Define the corpus of idiomatic expressions and figures of speech
	corpus := []string{
		"kick the bucket",
		"break a leg",
		"piece of cake",
		"raining cats and dogs",
		"hold your horses",
		"spill the beans",
	}
	// Define the labels for each expression
	labels := []string{
		"death",
		"good luck",
		"easy",
		"heavy rain",
		"wait",
		"reveal a secret",
	}
	// Train a Bayesian classifier on the corpus
	classifier := bayesian.NewClassifier(labels)
	for i, expression := range corpus {
		// Tokenize the expression
		tokens := strings.Split(expression, " ")
		// Stem the tokens using Porter stemming algorithm
		stemmedTokens := make([]string, len(tokens))
		for j, token := range tokens {
			stemmedTokens[j] = porter.Stem(token, english.Stemmer)
		}
		// Add the expression and label to the classifier
		classifier.Learn(stemmedTokens, labels[i])
	}
	// Classify a new expression
	newExpression := "it's raining cats and dogs outside"
	newTokens := strings.Split(newExpression, " ")
	newStemmedTokens := make([]string, len(newTokens))
	for i, token := range newTokens {
		newStemmedTokens[i] = porter.Stem(token, english.Stemmer)
	}
	probs, _, _ := classifier.LogScores(newStemmedTokens)
	maxIndex := bayesian.ArgMax(probs)
	fmt.Printf("The expression '%s' is classified as '%s'\n", newExpression, labels[maxIndex])
}
```
This code demonstrates how to train a Bayesian classifier on a corpus of idiomatic expressions and figures of speech, and use it to classify a new expression. The code also shows how to tokenize and stem the expressions using the Porter stemming algorithm.
When designing and deploying the algorithm in real-world scenarios, it is important to consider ethical considerations such as privacy, bias, and fairness. For example, the algorithm should not discriminate against certain populations or perpetuate harmful stereotypes. It is crucial to ensure that the data used to train the algorithm is diverse and representative of the population it will be deployed in. Additionally, appropriate measures should be taken to protect the privacy of individuals whose data is used to train the algorithm.

