To rank the movies based on user preferences and age range of the target audience equally, you can use a weighted scoring system. The steps to achieve this using C# code are as follows:
1. Define a class to represent a movie, with properties for the movie name, user preference score, and age range score:
```
public class Movie
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public int UserScore { get; set; }
    public int AgeScore { get; set; }
}
```
2. Create an array or list of Movie objects, and initialize them with the appropriate user preference and age range scores:
```
var movies = new List<Movie>
{
    new Movie { Name = "The Lord of the Rings", UserScore = 5, AgeScore = 3 },
    new Movie { Name = "Back to the Future", UserScore = 4, AgeScore = 4 },
    new Movie { Name = "The Matrix", UserScore = 3, AgeScore = 5 },
    new Movie { Name = "Jurassic Park", UserScore = 2, AgeScore = 4 }
};
```
3. Define the weights for user preference and age range, as well as the maximum score for each category:
```
const int UserWeight = 2;
const int AgeWeight = 1;
const int MaxUserScore = 5;
const int MaxAgeScore = 5;
```
4. Calculate the weighted score for each movie by multiplying the user preference score by the user weight, and the age range score by the age weight. Then, divide each score by the maximum score for that category to normalize the scores to a range of 0 to 1:
```
foreach (var movie in movies)
{
    double userScore = (double)movie.UserScore * UserWeight / MaxUserScore;
    double ageScore = (double)movie.AgeScore * AgeWeight / MaxAgeScore;
    movie.Score = userScore + ageScore;
}
```
5. Sort the movies by their final score in descending order:
```
movies.Sort((m1, m2) => m2.Score.CompareTo(m1.Score));
```
6. The sorted list of movies represents the final ranking, with the highest-scoring movie at the top:
```
foreach (var movie in movies)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"{movie.Name} - {movie.Score}");
}
```

