Here's the Java code to implement the function:
```
public static int[] getCommonElements(int[] arr1, int[] arr2) {
    // Create a HashSet to store unique common elements
    HashSet<Integer> set = new HashSet<Integer>();
    // Sort both arrays
    Arrays.sort(arr1);
    Arrays.sort(arr2);
    // Initialize two pointers to traverse both arrays
    int i = 0;
    int j = 0;
    // Traverse both arrays and add common elements to the HashSet
    while (i < arr1.length && j < arr2.length) {
        if (arr1[i] < arr2[j]) {
            i++;
        } else if (arr1[i] > arr2[j]) {
            j++;
        } else {
            set.add(arr1[i]);
            i++;
            j++;
        }
    }
    // Convert the HashSet to an array and return it
    int[] result = new int[set.size()];
    int k = 0;
    for (int num : set) {
        result[k++] = num;
    }
    return result;
}
```
This function first creates a HashSet to store the unique common elements between the two arrays. It then sorts both arrays and initializes two pointers to traverse them. It compares the elements at the current positions of the pointers and adds them to the HashSet if they are equal. The function then converts the HashSet to an array and returns it.

