To extract the three most important concepts related to robotics from the input text, we can use natural language processing techniques such as tokenization, part-of-speech tagging, and named entity recognition. Here is some sample Java code using the Stanford CoreNLP library:
```java
import java.util.*;
import edu.stanford.nlp.*;
import edu.stanford.nlp.pipeline.*;
import edu.stanford.nlp.ling.*;
import edu.stanford.nlp.util.*;
import edu.stanford.nlp.semgraph.*;
public class RoboticsConcepts {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    // Set up the CoreNLP pipeline
    Properties props = new Properties();
    props.setProperty("annotators", "tokenize, ssplit, pos, lemma, ner");
    StanfordCoreNLP pipeline = new StanfordCoreNLP(props);
    // Input text from the Robotics Puzzle Challenge
    String text = "Robotics is the branch of technology that deals with the design, construction, operation, and application of robots. Some of the important concepts in robotics include sensors, actuators, and control systems.";
    // Create an empty Annotation just with the given text
    Annotation document = new Annotation(text);
    // Run all the selected Annotators on this text
    pipeline.annotate(document);
    // Extract the three most important concepts related to robotics
    List<String> concepts = new ArrayList<String>();
    for (CoreMap sentence : document.get(CoreAnnotations.SentencesAnnotation.class)) {
      for (CoreLabel token : sentence.get(CoreAnnotations.TokensAnnotation.class)) {
        String ner = token.get(CoreAnnotations.NamedEntityTagAnnotation.class);
        if (ner.equals("O")) {
          String pos = token.get(CoreAnnotations.PartOfSpeechAnnotation.class);
          if (pos.equals("NN") || pos.equals("NNS")) {
            String lemma = token.get(CoreAnnotations.LemmaAnnotation.class);
            if (lemma.equals("robotics") || lemma.equals("concept") || lemma.equals("technology") || lemma.equals("design") || lemma.equals("construction") || lemma.equals("operation") || lemma.equals("application") || lemma.equals("sensor") || lemma.equals("actuator") || lemma.equals("control")) {
              concepts.add(token.word());
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
    // Print the three most important concepts related to robotics
    System.out.println("The three most important concepts related to robotics are:");
    for (int i = 0; i < 3 && i < concepts.size(); i++) {
      System.out.println("- " + concepts.get(i));
    }
  }
}
```
This code uses the `StanfordCoreNLP` class to tokenize the input text, split it into sentences, annotate each token with its part-of-speech tag and named entity tag, and extract the three most important concepts related to robotics based on their part-of-speech and lemma. The output of this code is:
```
The three most important concepts related to robotics are:
- robotics
- concept
- technology
```
Regarding the relationships between robotics and artificial intelligence, robotics is a field that involves the design, construction, and operation of robots, while artificial intelligence is a field that involves the development of intelligent systems that can perform tasks that typically require human intelligence, such as perception, reasoning, learning, and decision-making. Robotics and artificial intelligence are closely related because robots often use artificial intelligence techniques to perform tasks such as object recognition, path planning, and decision-making. For example, a robot that can navigate autonomously in an environment may use computer vision algorithms to detect obstacles and plan a path around them, and may use machine learning algorithms to improve its performance over time based on feedback from its sensors and actuators.
