Can you provide a [Java code] implementation that uses the Merge Sort algorithm to sort an input array of integers in ascending order? The input array is as follows:
int[] arr = {3, 9, 4, 1, 6, 7};
The sorted array should look like this:
{1, 3, 4, 6, 7, 9}
Please ensure that your implementation has a time complexity of O(nlog(n)) and uses a divide-and-conquer approach. Additionally, please provide comments to explain your implementation for clarity.

output:
Sure, here is a Java code implementation that uses the Merge Sort algorithm to sort an input array of integers in ascending order:
```
public class MergeSort {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {3, 9, 4, 1, 6, 7};
        mergeSort(arr, 0, arr.length - 1);
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(arr));
    }

    public static void mergeSort(int[] arr, int left, int right) {
        if (left < right) {
            int mid = (left + right) / 2;
            mergeSort(arr, left, mid); // sort left half
            mergeSort(arr, mid + 1, right); // sort right half
            merge(arr, left, mid, right); // merge sorted halves
        }
    }

    public static void merge(int[] arr, int left, int mid, int right) {
        int[] temp = new int[right - left + 1]; // temp array to hold sorted elements
        int i = left; // index for left half of array
        int j = mid + 1; // index for right half of array
        int k = 0; // index for temp array

        // merge elements in sorted order
        while (i <= mid && j <= right) {
            if (arr[i] <= arr[j]) {
                temp[k++] = arr[i++];
            } else {
                temp[k++] = arr[j++];
            }
        }

        // copy remaining elements from left half to temp array
        while (i <= mid) {
            temp[k++] = arr[i++];
        }

        // copy remaining elements from right half to temp array
        while (j <= right) {
            temp[k++] = arr[j++];
        }

        // copy sorted elements from temp array back to original array
        for (i = left; i <= right; i++) {
            arr[i] = temp[i - left];
        }
    }
}
```
Explanation:
The `mergeSort` method recursively divides the input array into two halves until each half contains only one element. Then, it calls the `merge` method to merge the sorted halves into a single sorted array.
The `merge` method creates a temporary array to hold the sorted elements. It then compares the elements in the left and right halves of the input array and adds the smaller element to the temporary array. If there are remaining elements in either half, they are copied to the temporary array. Finally, the sorted elements in the temporary array are copied back to the original input array.
The time complexity of Merge Sort is O(nlog(n)), where n is the number of elements in the input array. This is because the input array is divided into halves log(n) times, and each division takes O(n) time to merge the sorted halves.

